-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  float_conv.adam - Float conversions (M3.6d), C's rules. Every integer
  width to float64 at its limits (int64 max rounds up to 2^63, uint64 max
  to 2^64, 2^63 + 1025 to 2^63 + 2048: the halving keeps its low bit, so
  it rounds as the whole number would), a small uint64 and a bool; integers
  to float32 rounded once (16777217 to 16777216, 2^63 + 2^39 + 1 up to
  2^63 + 2^40, never through float64); floats to every integer width
  truncated toward zero, at the int64 / uint64 limits (2^63 and the largest
  float64 below 2^64 into a uint64, from float64 and float32); float64 to
  float32 rounded, float32 to float64 exact, an overflow to +inf, a float32
  NaN widened with its payload, -0.0 kept; casts folded at compile time
  (int32(2.75), a uint64 above 2^63, bool(0.0) and bool(0.5) true, as C's
  `x != 0`). A float shows as its IEEE bits through an overlay, an integer
  in decimal (_i64toa / _ui64toa). Expected lines:
  scripts\adam\float_conv_expect.py.
*******************************************************************************/

/*
